# Hamlaneh edge proxy configuration.
#
# The site address resolves from HAMLANEH_DOMAIN (default "localhost"):
#   - "localhost" or a bare IP  -> certificate from Caddy's internal CA
#     (browsers warn until the CA cert, stored in the caddy_data volume,
#     is trusted — documented in the install docs)
#   - a real domain             -> automatic publicly trusted certificates
#     via ACME, plus HTTP->HTTPS redirect
# Switching between the two needs no edits here — only the env var.
#
# On bare IPs, measured rather than assumed: Let's Encrypt has issued
# certificates for IP addresses since January 2026, but only under its
# short-lived profile, and Caddy does not request them. Nor does Caddy issue
# locally for a PUBLIC IP on its own — only loopback and localhost count as
# internal to it; a public address got a TLS site with no certificate at
# all. That is why the global block below names the issuer explicitly.
#
# Note: this file is baked into the proxy image (Dockerfile.caddy).
# After editing it, re-run: docker compose up -d --build

# A client that connects by IP address sends no SNI — the TLS extension
# that names the site — because SNI is defined for hostnames only. With two
# certificates in its cache (the app's and the files origin's) and no name
# to pick by, Caddy answers such a handshake with a TLS "internal error"
# alert, and a bare-IP install is unreachable from every browser (measured
# on the first custom-port install; latent for bare IP on 443 too). This
# names the certificate to serve when no name arrives: the app's own host,
# written by install.sh without any port. For a domain install it is inert,
# since browsers always send SNI for a hostname.
{
	default_sni {$HAMLANEH_DEFAULT_SNI:localhost}
	# Who issues the site's certificate. Caddy issues locally, unasked, only
	# for names it classes as internal — localhost and loopback; a PUBLIC IP
	# address is neither internal nor something a public CA will be asked
	# for, so an install on one got a TLS site with no certificate behind it
	# and every handshake ended in an "internal error" alert (measured, twice,
	# after the scheme and SNI fixes). install.sh writes "internal" for an
	# IP or localhost install and "acme" for a domain; the default here is
	# what Caddy would do on its own for a domain.
	cert_issuer {$HAMLANEH_CERT_ISSUER:acme}
	# HTTP/3 rides QUIC, and browsers refuse QUIC to a certificate they do
	# not trust — there is no "proceed anyway" for it, as there is for TLS
	# over TCP. So on an install whose certificate is locally issued (an IP,
	# localhost), advertising h3 is a trap: the first page loads over TCP,
	# the browser learns the Alt-Svc, tries QUIC for the next request, and
	# the sign-in fetch dies with "could not reach the server" while curl
	# from anywhere says 200. install.sh writes "h1 h2" for those installs
	# and the full set for a domain, whose certificate every browser trusts.
	servers {
		protocols {$HAMLANEH_HTTP_PROTOCOLS:h1 h2 h3}
	}
}

# The https:// is load-bearing, not decoration. Without a scheme, Caddy
# only applies automatic HTTPS to a site on its default HTTPS port — an
# address carrying a custom port (the "443 was taken" installs write
# HAMLANEH_DOMAIN as ip:port) came up as a PLAINTEXT HTTP server, measured:
# TLS handshakes died with "wrong version number" while plain HTTP got an
# answer on the same port. The explicit scheme makes every form of the
# address — localhost, bare IP, domain, ip:port — a TLS site; for the
# portless forms it changes nothing.
https://{$HAMLANEH_DOMAIN:localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# HEADER OWNERSHIP — read before adding anything here.
	#
	# This proxy sets only what a TLS terminator uniquely owns: HSTS (which
	# is meaningless without TLS, and Caddy is what has it) and hiding the
	# server software. The application's content-security headers — CSP,
	# X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy and the
	# Cross-Origin-* pair — belong to the Go server and are set there, in
	# server/internal/httpserver/securityheaders.go.
	#
	# Two reasons, both learned the hard way elsewhere:
	#   1. Browsers AND multiple Content-Security-Policy headers. A second
	#      copy here that drifts from the app's produces a third policy
	#      nobody wrote and nobody can debug from either file.
	#   2. Home mode is a single binary with no proxy at all (CLAUDE.md,
	#      "Packaging"). A policy that lives only here does not exist for
	#      those installs.
	#
	# So: do not "helpfully" add CSP or its neighbours back. If the policy is
	# wrong, fix it in the Go server, where the code that knows what the app
	# loads also lives.
	header {
		# 180 days.
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=15552000"
		# Do not advertise server software.
		-Server
	}

	# The LiveKit signal path (ADR 005). Calls ride the app origin instead of
	# a subdomain of their own: no second certificate, nothing for the client
	# to configure, bare-IP installs work identically, and the existing
	# connect-src 'self' already admits a same-origin wss: — so the app's CSP
	# needs no exception for calls.
	#
	# SECURITY INVARIANT — this matcher is an allow-list of exactly the two
	# signal routes, never a prefix of the whole service. LiveKit serves its
	# RoomService admin API on /twirp/*, and goroutine and room dumps on
	# /debug/*, from the SAME port. Widening this to `path /rtc*` or, worse,
	# proxying the site root to livekit would publish an unauthenticated-by-
	# our-standards admin surface on the public origin. RoomService is
	# reachable only from inside the compose network, by the server that
	# holds the API secret.
	@rtc path /rtc /rtc/*
	handle @rtc {
		# WebSocket upgrades need no extra directive: reverse_proxy passes
		# them through. The join token is a query parameter on this URL and
		# never a cookie, which is why nothing here forwards credentials.
		reverse_proxy livekit:7880
	}

	# SECURITY INVARIANT — this matcher is the app port's half of ADR 015: the
	# powered admin surface must be GONE from here, not merely unlinked. The
	# surfaces with power are /api/v1/admin/* and /scim/v2/*, and an operator
	# who shut the admin port in their cloud firewall has to be right about
	# that. Nothing may be added here that answers instead of vanishing.
	#
	# The PAGE half below is deliberately friendlier, and it is not an
	# exception to this: /admin is a client-side route that renders nothing
	# until the API answers (docs/hardening.md), so 404ing it would protect
	# nothing and a redirect discloses nothing a port scan does not.
	#
	# The server is the enforcement point (ADR 015: "in the server, not only
	# in the proxy") — home mode has no proxy at all. This is the second lock,
	# not the only one.
	#
	# It is a variable rather than a literal because the split is OFF by
	# default: with no admin port configured every admin route still belongs
	# here, exactly as it did before ADR 015, and a --non-interactive install
	# must not change meaning. install.sh writes the expression when the
	# operator moves the surface; the default below matches no path anything
	# will ever request. Widening it to /api/v1/* would 404 the whole API.
	@adminmoved path_regexp {$HAMLANEH_ADMIN_MOVED_PATHS:/__admin-has-not-moved__}
	handle @adminmoved {
		respond 404
	}

	# The page half: /admin and everything bookmarked under it move too, but
	# they are sent on rather than denied. This is what lets the sidebar's
	# Admin control stay a plain "/admin" — the client needs no deployment
	# fact, so the admin port never has to be published in a document served
	# to anonymous visitors. {uri} carries the pane with it, so a bookmark to
	# /admin/audit still lands on /admin/audit.
	#
	# On a loopback-bound admin port this redirect goes somewhere the public
	# app cannot reach, and that is what "this machine only" means; the
	# installer says so when that answer is given. A correct absolute URL
	# would have been just as dead.
	@adminpage path_regexp {$HAMLANEH_ADMIN_PAGE_PATHS:/__admin-has-not-moved__}
	handle @adminpage {
		redir https://{$HAMLANEH_DEFAULT_SNI:localhost}:{$HAMLANEH_ADMIN_PORT:9443}{uri}
	}

	# SECURITY INVARIANT — do not add trusted_proxies here without changing
	# the Go server. With no trusted_proxies (this default), Caddy strips any
	# client-supplied X-Forwarded-For and forwards only the true peer IP; the
	# server's rate-limiter IP extraction (internal/httpserver clientIP)
	# trusts the leftmost XFF value on that basis. Fronting Caddy with a CDN
	# or another proxy requires switching the server to rightmost-untrusted
	# parsing first, or login rate limiting becomes spoofable.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy server:8080
	}
}

# The admin origin (ADR 015). The dashboard and its API move to their own
# PORT on the same host, because a cloud firewall filters by port and not by
# path — "do not open this port" is a control the operator already has, and it
# works upstream of the host where no matcher in this file can reach.
#
# The host is deliberately the app's own, port stripped: the same name, so
# nothing new enters Caddy's certificate cache and the default_sni/cert_issuer
# machinery a bare-IP install depends on is untouched. A second HOSTNAME would
# have re-run ADR 003's failure and, being a different origin, would have taken
# the session cookie away — cookies here are host-only and are NOT port-scoped,
# which is the whole reason a port works and a hostname does not.
#
# HAMLANEH_DEFAULT_SNI is reused rather than duplicated: it is already "the
# app's host with any custom port removed", written by install.sh for every
# install, and a second variable holding the same thing is a second thing to
# drift.
https://{$HAMLANEH_DEFAULT_SNI:localhost}:{$HAMLANEH_ADMIN_PORT:9443} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Same ownership rule as the app site: HSTS and hiding the server
	# software are all this proxy sets. HSTS is host-scoped rather than
	# origin-scoped, so this and the app site are talking about the same
	# host — they must not disagree about the max-age.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=15552000"
		-Server
	}

	# SECURITY INVARIANT — this matcher is an allow-list of exactly the admin
	# surface, never a prefix of the whole service. The port exists so that
	# closing it closes the dashboard and SCIM; proxying the site root here
	# would put the entire application on a port an operator was told to treat
	# as the admin one, and every path they believed they had shut — sign-in,
	# the WebSocket, /files/* — would answer on it.
	#
	# The first four are what MOVED — the main listener loses them. The rest
	# are SHARED, carried by both listeners, because the admin listener is a
	# complete minimal app rather than a bare admin surface (server.go,
	# sharedSurface): the page has to load its bundle, learn what this instance
	# is, learn who is signed in, and — for the operator who bound this port to
	# loopback and reaches it through an SSH tunnel, having never opened the
	# chat port at all — sign in on it and clear every mandatory gate on it,
	# the forced password change and two-step enrolment alike. A gate with no
	# exit on this port strands that operator exactly as a missing bootstrap
	# call would.
	#
	# This list has to mirror adminListenerServes. It has drifted behind it
	# twice already, and both times the symptom was a dashboard that could not
	# finish loading rather than anything that looked like a proxy problem —
	# so install.test.sh pins it, and a path added to sharedSurface has to be
	# added here in the same change.
	#
	# The upstream is the server's SECOND listener; the port below must match
	# the port in HAMLANEH_ADMIN_ADDR, which install.sh writes as :9090.
	#
	# The port is a DEPLOYMENT boundary and never an authorization decision
	# (ADR 015). Reaching it is not being an admin: the same session and the
	# same authz.Can call refuse everyone else, exactly as on the app port —
	# which is why sharing the sign-in routes confers nothing.
	@adminsurface path /admin /admin/* /api/v1/admin/* /scim/v2/* /api/v1/instance /api/v1/users/me /api/v1/users/me/totp* /api/v1/auth/* /assets/* /brand/*
	handle @adminsurface {
		reverse_proxy server:9090
	}

	# "/" on this port means two opposite things, and the first operator to
	# open the admin origin met the wrong one: they typed the bare host:port,
	# which can only mean "show me the dashboard", and the catch-all below
	# sent them to the chat app instead. Nothing else lives on this port for
	# them to have meant.
	#
	# So the two readings are told apart by a marker the dashboard's own exit
	# control sets, rather than by guessing:
	#
	#   /?to=chat   clicked Back to chat from inside the dashboard -> the app
	#   /           typed, bookmarked, or followed from anywhere else -> /admin
	#
	# The marker is on the way OUT because that is the caller we control; the
	# way in is whatever a person types, and it gets the useful answer. Both
	# targets are fixed — one a configured host, one a literal path — so
	# neither reads anything from the request and there is no open redirect.
	@exit {
		path /
		query to=chat
	}
	handle @exit {
		redir https://{$HAMLANEH_DOMAIN:localhost}/
	}

	@dashboard path /
	handle @dashboard {
		# The `*` is required, not decoration: a first argument beginning with
		# "/" is read as a path matcher, so `redir /admin` parses as a matcher
		# with no destination and Caddy refuses the file outright.
		redir * /admin
	}

	# Everything else on this port belongs to the app, so say so instead of
	# answering 404: a chat route reached here would paint a shell whose every
	# call is cross-origin and refused. The target is a fixed configured host,
	# never anything from the request, so there is no open redirect here.
	handle {
		redir https://{$HAMLANEH_DOMAIN:localhost}{uri}
	}
}

# The files origin (ADR 003). Uploaded bytes are served from a SEPARATE
# hostname, routed to the same Go binary, so that a file can never script
# against the app: a different origin means no cookies are sent with the
# request, no app document shares a scripting context with it, and the
# signed URL is therefore the only credential involved.
#
# The separate origin is defense in depth, NOT the defense. Every file
# response carries Content-Disposition: attachment (for anything that is not
# a proven image), X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff and a sandboxing CSP, set
# by the Go server, on every serving mode — see files_origin.go. An install
# that never reaches this block still gets all three.
#
# Bare-IP and localhost installs: "files.<ip>" is not a name anything can
# certify meaningfully, and home mode has no proxy at all. Those installs
# serve /files/* from the app origin instead, with the identical headers.
# That is why deploy/verify-defaults.sh probes the app origin unconditionally
# and this hostname only when the domain is a real one.
#
# The site address is its OWN variable rather than "files." glued onto
# HAMLANEH_DOMAIN, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness. Glued to a
# bare IP it produced "files.203.0.113.7" -- not an IP, so Caddy could not use
# the internal CA, and not a name anyone controls, so ACME could never
# succeed. Caddy retried that for max_duration 2592000: a month of failing
# certificate orders on an install that was otherwise working, from a
# hostname the deployment had already decided not to use.
#
# The default is a .localhost name, which Caddy certifies from its internal
# CA without touching the network, so a deployment that never sets the
# variable declares an inert block instead of an impossible one. install.sh
# sets it to files.<domain> only when it was given a real domain.
{$HAMLANEH_FILES_DOMAIN:files.localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Same ownership rule as above: HSTS and hiding the server software are
	# all this proxy sets. The file responses' own security headers come
	# from the Go server, which is the only thing that knows whether the
	# bytes it is about to write proved to be an image.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=15552000"
		-Server
	}

	# Only the two file routes exist here. Everything else on this hostname
	# is 404 rather than a second copy of the application: the whole point
	# of the origin is that it serves nothing that could script.
	@files path /files/*
	handle @files {
		reverse_proxy server:8080
	}
	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}
